Chemotherapy-Induced Peripheral Neuropathy (CIPN)
Haematology & Oncology
Stocking-glove tingling/numbness, Burning/shooting neuropathic pain, History of neurotoxic chemotherapy, Reduced SNAP amplitudes on nerve conduction studies

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-010
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, unmanageable neuropathic pain and a subsequent mechanical fall secondary to profound sensory ataxia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient on neurotoxic chemotherapy who sustained a fall in the dark due to a sudden loss of feeling in the feet, complicated by agonizing, shooting leg pain unresponsive to oral analgesia. Urgent physical assessment was mandated to rule out post-fall fractures, assess for acute spinal cord compression, and provide immediate pharmacological pain relief, which could not wait until the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My feet have been numb for weeks, but tonight the burning pain shot up my legs so badly I lost my balance in the dark and crashed into the wall."
History of Present Illness: The patient recently completed a course of oxaliplatin-based chemotherapy. They report a progressive, symmetrical bilateral tingling and numbness beginning in the toes (stocking-glove distribution) over the last month. Tonight, the patient experienced a severe, sudden onset of burning, shooting neuropathic pain in both lower extremities. When attempting to walk to the bathroom in the dark, profound sensory ataxia led to an unsteady gait, resulting in a stumble and fall against the hallway wall. The patient reports extreme sensitivity to the cold floor. No head strike or loss of consciousness.
Functional Status: Currently immobile on the floor/low chair, refusing to weight-bear due to severe neuropathic pain and fear of falling.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; high risk of recurrent falls and unnoticed trauma due to sensory loss.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 102 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Distressed adult, guarding lower limbs. Haemodynamically stable.
Physical Exam:
Neurological: Cranial nerves intact. Upper limb strength 5/5. Lower limb strength 5/5, but movement is hesitant due to pain. Diminished deep tendon reflexes (areflexia) at the ankles and knees bilaterally. Severe loss of light touch, pinprick, and vibration sensation in a stocking-glove distribution up to the mid-calves. Romberg test positive (severe sensory ataxia with eyes closed).
Musculoskeletal: No bony tenderness over the spine, hips, or knees post-fall. Full, passive range of motion in lower limbs without joint pain.
Skin: No unnoticed burns, cuts, or trauma on the plantar surfaces of the feet.
Environmental Observations: Dim lighting in the hallway; loose rugs present.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking paracetamol 1g QID with zero efficacy for the burning pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chemotherapy-Induced Peripheral Neuropathy (CIPN) - Status: Exacerbated (Pain Crisis and Fall)
Differentials:

Acute Spinal Cord Compression - Ruled out/unlikely due to: preserved bilateral motor strength, absence of bowel/bladder dysfunction, and lack of localized spinal tenderness.

Post-Fall Fracture (Neck of Femur/Pelvis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: painless passive range of motion of the hips and knees, lack of limb shortening or external rotation, and ability to move legs against gravity.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: bilateral, symmetrical nature of the symptoms, lack of unilateral swelling or erythema, and the distinctly neuropathic character of the pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute crisis of neuropathic pain and a resultant fall driven by sensory ataxia, directly linked to recent neurotoxic chemotherapy exposure. The severe burning pain is completely refractory to simple analgesia, causing significant distress and immobility. A home visit was urgently required to clinically clear the patient of major traumatic fractures or acute spinal cord emergencies post-fall. Furthermore, leaving the patient in agonizing pain overnight without initiating targeted neuropathic agents would be clinically inappropriate and inhumane, satisfying the criteria for urgent after-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered immediate oral pregabalin 75mg stat for acute neuropathic pain suppression. Advised against mobilizing in the dark. Planned outpatient referral for formal nerve conduction studies to document reduced SNAP amplitudes.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Always turn on bright lights before standing up at night. By using your vision to compensate for the lost sensory feedback from your feet, you drastically reduce your risk of falling.

Wear properly fitted, hard-soled shoes inside the house at all times. By protecting your feet with a physical barrier, you prevent unnoticed cuts or trauma to your numb skin.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral for home physiotherapy and occupational therapy for mobility aids.
DME/Safety (Optional): Nightlights for hallways; shower chair.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to titrate the new neuropathic pain medication and review fall safety.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if you develop new weakness or inability to move your legs, loss of bowel or bladder control, or if you fall again and cannot get up.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The examination indicates nerve damage from the chemotherapy; we have ruled out broken bones tonight, but the fall risk remains high.
Clinical Course: The nerve damage causes both pain and a lack of balance. The new medication will take a few days to build up in your system and calm the burning nerves.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not walk in the dark tonight. If you need to use the bathroom, turn on all lights and use the walls for support.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Normal painkillers like paracetamol do not work on nerve pain. The new medication (pregabalin) works directly on the overactive nerves to stop them from sending shooting pain signals to your brain.
Actionable Steps: Take the new medication exactly as prescribed. Keep a lamp on tonight. Check the bottoms of your feet visually every day for cuts.

